---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RuleRef from '../components/RuleRef.astro';
import { ruleByKey, ruleCountWord } from '../data/rules.js';
import { lessonCount, levels } from '../data/curriculum.js';
// The guiding principle quoted below IS rule 03 — pulled from rules.js rather than
// restated, so a reworded rule can never leave /about arguing for the old one.
const simplest = ruleByKey('simplest-setup');
---
<Base
  title="About — how this guide works · BitcoinKeys.guide"
  description="BitcoinKeys.guide is an independent, Bitcoin-only self-custody guide. We hold no keys, sell no device, and take no affiliate money. Here's how it works and why you can trust it."
  verified="2026-08-04"
>
  <section class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>An honest guide, because we have <span class="accent">nothing to sell</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">Almost every self-custody guide is written by someone with a product in the frame — a hardware brand, a custody service, an exchange, an affiliate payout. This one isn’t.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>The pledge</h2>
    <div class="pledges">
      <div class="pl">
        <span class="k mono">No device to sell</span>
        <p>We don’t make or sell hardware wallets, and we take no affiliate commission on any device. So when the guide rates a device against <a href="/standard">our standard</a> — or says one wallet fits you better than another — there’s no money bending the answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pl">
        <span class="k mono">No keys held</span>
        <p>We never touch your coins, your seed, or your keys. This is a guide, full stop — not a wallet, not a custody service, not an app that holds anything. The whole point is that <em>you</em> hold your Bitcoin.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pl">
        <span class="k mono">Bitcoin only</span>
        <p>No altcoins, no “stablecoin yield,” no DeFi. Those aren’t self-custody — they’re distractions that get people hurt. We stay on one thing: holding Bitcoin well.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pl">
        <span class="k mono">Dated and in the open</span>
        <p>Hardware, firmware, and best practices drift. Every page shows the date its guidance was last checked against reality, so you always know how fresh it is — no undated screenshots from three years ago.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>How we think about advice</h2>
    <p><strong>The premise underneath everything here: security is something you learn and practise, and there is nothing you can buy that does it for you.</strong> No device, no service and no arrangement is safe on its own — what actually keeps Bitcoin is a person who understands their own setup and has rehearsed it. That is why this is a <a href="/start">course</a> before it is a set of tools, and why we would rather you understood one simple setup completely than owned an impressive one you have never tested.</p>
    <p><strong>It is also why our advice leans conservative, and that lean is deliberate rather than temperamental.</strong> Attacks only get cheaper and better, and they are now being automated faster than the guidance warning people about them. A shortcut that is merely risky today is the one found at scale tomorrow; anything resting on obscurity, convenience or good luck is on a clock. So the test we apply to a recommendation is not <em>is this safe now</em> — it is <em>is this still standing in ten years, operated by a real person having a bad week</em>. Almost everything below follows from that.</p>
    <p>The whole guide rests on <a href="/learn/rules">{ruleCountWord} rules</a>. They’re the first thing the course teaches, they take two minutes to read, and every lesson after that exists to explain one of them in full. Follow the {ruleCountWord} and you have done the important part.</p>
    <p>The rule that shapes the <em>advice</em> most is <RuleRef key="simplest-setup" />: <strong>{simplest.rule}</strong> Not the most impressive setup, and not the one that sells the most gear. Most people are safest on the lower rungs of the <a href="/learn/ladder">ladder</a> and should stay there until a real reason moves them up. We’ll tell you when <em>not</em> to add complexity as often as when to — and <a href="/find-your-setup">finding your setup</a> is built the same way, so it will hold you back from one you don’t need.</p>
    <p><strong>The one thing that rule does not excuse is a single point of failure.</strong> Simplest does not mean one wallet, one seed, no passphrase, and everything you own depending on that one thing being fine. When a firmware flaw made <a href="/advisory/coldcard-seed-entropy">weak Coldcard seeds</a> guessable in 2026, every wallet drained was exactly that arrangement — and the holders who came through untouched had a passphrase, their own dice throws, or a key from a second maker. So for money whose loss would genuinely hurt, our recommendation always clears that bar, and <a href="/how-we-weigh-risk">the finder is checked on every path it can produce</a> rather than merely promising it. Below that — Bitcoin you’re learning with, money whose loss wouldn’t change your life — one wallet kept properly is still an honest answer, and we’ll say so.</p>
    <p>We’re confidently pro-self-custody. Holding your own keys is a skill worth learning, and the risks are manageable once you can name them. But this is your money and your responsibility — nothing here is financial or legal advice, and you should never act on a step you don’t understand. When something is safety-critical, we say so loudly.</p>

    <h2>Who’s behind it</h2>
    {/* The old version explained the pen name at length
        — "not evasion… shouldn't require the author to paint a target on their own
        back" — which foregrounds the very thing it is there to keep unremarkable, and
        reads as a personal threat model on a page about a website. That is rule 10
        ("Talk about Bitcoin. Never talk about your Bitcoin.") applied to ourselves.
        State it once, plainly, and move to what actually earns trust: checkability. */}
    <p>BitcoinKeys.guide is a <strong>RadVladdy</strong> project — one of a small family of independent Bitcoin sites published under that name. What matters here is whether the advice is right, which is why every page carries the date it was last checked and every claim is one you can go and verify yourself.</p>

    <h2>Where the material comes from</h2>
    <p>The guidance is distilled from the strongest practitioner sources in the field — Jameson Lopp’s two decades of security work, Blockchain Commons’ Smart Custody framework, and the published operational guidance from Unchained, Nunchuk, and others — then re-checked and written in plain English. Where those sources disagree, we say so rather than pretending there’s one answer.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">Kept current, in the open</span>
      <p>The guide is complete and actively maintained — the {lessonCount}-lesson course across {levels.length} levels, the configuration ladder, the wallet comparison, the tools, and the interactive demos are all live. Prices and specs get re-checked on a schedule, and every change is written down in the <a href="/changelog">what’s-changed log</a>. New here? <a href="/start">Start here</a> — the whole guide laid out in the order it makes sense.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .pledges { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem; margin: 1.4rem 0; }
  @media (max-width: 700px) { .pledges { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .pl { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 0.6rem; padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem; background: var(--surface); }
  .pl .k { display: block; color: var(--accent); font-size: 0.74rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 0.45rem; }
  .pl p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--muted); }
</style>
